        "heading": "3.2 ADVERSARIAL LEARNING WITH STRUCTURAL OBJECTIVES",
        "text": "Our goal is to task D as the feature extractor to learn data representation on real images. We denote the p-dimensional output from D of real images and fake generations as f , fg respectively. Here f ,fg \u2208 Sp\u22121 are normalized and live in a unit hypersphere. We also maintain the unnormalized counterparts f\u0303 and f\u0303g of f and fg , where we explore their utility in Section 3.3.\nOur proposed structural objectives operate hierarchically and regularize the learned embeddings at two levels of granularity: (1) At a coarse level, we assume both f ,fg follow Gaussian distributions: f \u223c N (\u00b5f ,\u03a3f ),fg \u223c N (\u00b5fg ,\u03a3fg ), where \u00b5f ,\u00b5fg \u2208 Rp and \u03a3f ,\u03a3fg \u2208 Rp\u00d7p are sample mean and covariance respectively. (2) At a refined level, we focus on reorganizing embeddings by constructing clusters using local affinity. Therefore, by distributing our objective function with dedicated geometric patterns, we further aim to reinforce the feature learning capability of D under the purely unsupervised setting.\nCoarse-scale Optimization with Gaussian. Under unimodal Gaussian assumptions, we can simultaneously control the geometry and locations of feature distributions using simple empirical statistics, i.e., sample mean and covariance. With a selected metric d(\u00b7) for Gaussian distributions, we define our goal as a minimax problem:\nLGaussian := min G max D d(f ,fg). (2)\nOne widely adopted candidate for d(\u00b7) is Jensen\u2013Shannon divergence (JSD) due to its symmetry and stability. For two arbitrary probability distributions P,Q, JSD admits the following form:\nJSD(P ||Q) = DKL(P || P +Q\n2 ) +DKL(Q||\nP +Q\n2 ) = H(\nP +Q 2 )\u2212 1 2 (H(P ) +H(Q)) , (3)\nwhere DKL, H denotes Kullback\u2013Leibler divergence and entropy respectively. We can compute entropy for Q,P using closed-form formulas. However, entropy for (P + Q)/2 is problematic to compute exactly and generally requires Monte 